====================================================================================================================
g01 -- does modified INERTIA predict a different SIGN for the external-field slope?  Short answer: no.
====================================================================================================================
  N = 92 Local Group dwarfs with a MEASURED dispersion (45 MW, 34 M31, 13 field).  Local Volume Database (Pace 2024).

====================================================================================================================
1.  THE HOPE'S PREMISE, TESTED: does going to the freely-falling frame remove the effect in modified inertia?
====================================================================================================================
  Local ('algebraic') modified inertia: the body's TOTAL acceleration a in the inertial frame obeys
      mu(|a|/a_0) a = -grad Phi_N   ==>   a = nu(|g_N|/a_0) g_N   pointwise  (Milgrom 1994; 2011).
  The dwarf's centre of mass obeys the SAME law with the host's field, A_cm = nu(g_Ne/a_0) g_Ne, so the
  relative acceleration a_rel = a - A_cm is what an internal observer measures.  If the equivalence principle
  were intact, a_rel would not know g_Ne at all.  Sphere-average -<a_rel . r_hat>/h and compare with QUMOND's
  flux-theorem answer <g_r> = <S_r>, S = nu(|g_N|/a_0) g_N (f01).  TWO INDEPENDENT CODE PATHS.

        x_e      h/a_0    MI  -<a_rel.r>/h    QUMOND  -<S_r>/h    frac diff   nu(x_e)(1+L/3)
     0.0010   1.00e-06           26.855388           26.855388      0.0000%        26.855388
     0.0010   1.00e-05           26.855323           26.855323     -0.0000%        26.855388
     0.0100   1.00e-05            8.843053            8.843053     -0.0000%         8.843053
     0.0100   1.00e-04            8.843033            8.843033      0.0000%         8.843053
     0.1000   1.00e-04            3.165910            3.165910     -0.0000%         3.165910
     0.1000   1.00e-03            3.165904            3.165904      0.0000%         3.165910
     0.5000   5.00e-04            1.746532            1.746532     -0.0000%         1.746532
     0.5000   5.00e-03            1.746529            1.746529      0.0000%         1.746532
     1.0000   1.00e-03            1.428531            1.428531     -0.0000%         1.428531
     1.0000   1.00e-02            1.428530            1.428530      0.0000%         1.428531
  [PASS] G1 (AGAINST THE HOPE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF SECTION 1) local modified inertia gives the SAME sphere-averaged external-field coupling as QUMOND, to quadrature precision, by two independent code paths. Going to the freely-falling frame does NOT remove the external-field effect, because mu depends on the acceleration MAGNITUDE and a_0 is absolute: MOND of every kind breaks the strong equivalence principle. The centre-of-mass term drops out only because its radial sphere-average vanishes, which is computed here and not assumed.   (worst fractional MI-vs-QUMOND difference over 10 (x_e, h) pairs = 0.00000%; |<A_cm . r_hat>| <= 1.73e-18)

====================================================================================================================
2.  SO THE ESCAPE MUST BE NONLOCAL.  One parameter: the external-field transparency w.
====================================================================================================================
  Modified inertia is a CLASS, not a theory.  Milgrom 1994 shows the modification must be a NONLOCAL
  functional of the whole trajectory (a local one cannot conserve momentum), and nothing in the class fixes
  how much a zero-frequency, constant external acceleration weighs against the system's own bound modes.
  So parameterise it honestly with ONE number and report the RANGE:
      log C(w) = log nu_sphere(x_int) + w * [ log nu_sphere(x_int + x_e) - log nu_sphere(x_int) ],  x = g_N/a_0
      w = 1  local MI == modified gravity (section 1); w = 0  full equivalence-principle restoration, no EFE.
  w is NOT derived here and MUST NOT be quoted as a prediction.  It is a dial spanning the class's freedom.
  (The transparency is applied in the LOG of the coupling, not inside the kernel's argument.  Putting it in the
   argument sends x_int + w x_e negative for w < 0 where the kernel is undefined -- a bug that produced slopes
   of order +30 in the first run of this script, and is recorded here rather than quietly fixed.)

  ---- canonical footing, a_0 = 9.360e-11 ------------------------------------------------------------
  g_e/a_0 spans 0.0070 to 4.876, median 0.0931
  MEASURED slope, statistic (C), identical design matrix: +0.0800 +/- 0.0466
           w  arm                                           predicted slope   EFE part   sigma from data
